# LinkedIn URN via PowerShell — Full Guide

---

## What is this?
LinkedIn uses OAuth 2.0 to authenticate users. To interact with the LinkedIn API (post content, get profile info, etc.) you need an **access token**. Your **URN** (Uniform Resource Name) is your unique LinkedIn member ID, looks like `urn:li:person:XXXXXXX`. You need this for automation tools like n8n.

---

## What you need before starting

**1. LinkedIn Developer Account**
- Go to linkedin.com/developers
- Sign in with your LinkedIn account

**2. Create or open your App**
- Go to linkedin.com/developers/apps
- Click your app (or create new one)
- Go to **Auth** tab
- You will find your **Client ID** and **Client Secret** here

**3. Add your Redirect URI**
- Still on the **Auth** tab
- Under "OAuth 2.0 settings" → "Authorized Redirect URLs"
- Add your redirect URI exactly, e.g. `https://auto.devdabs.com/rest/oauth2-credential/callback`
- This must match exactly what you use in the commands — even a trailing slash will break it

**4. Add required OAuth scopes**
- Go to **Products** tab in your app
- Request access to **Sign In with LinkedIn using OpenID Connect**
- This gives you: `openid`, `profile`, `email`
- For posting: also request **Share on LinkedIn** which gives `w_member_social`

---

## Step 1 — Open the Authorization URL

Run this in PowerShell (replace YOUR_CLIENT_ID and YOUR_REDIRECT_URI_ENCODED):
```powershell
Start-Process "https://www.linkedin.com/oauth/v2/authorization?response_type=code&client_id=YOUR_CLIENT_ID&redirect_uri=YOUR_REDIRECT_URI_ENCODED&scope=openid%20profile%20email%20w_member_social&state=123456"
```

**What happens:**
- Browser opens LinkedIn login page
- You log in and click Allow/Authorize
- LinkedIn redirects you to your redirect URI
- The page may show an error — that is fine and expected
- Look at the browser address bar

**The URL will look like:**
```
https://your-redirect-uri/callback?code=AQTcbLtV3x......&state=123456
```

**Copy the code value** — everything after `code=` and before `&state`

⚠️ This code expires in **60 seconds** — move to Step 2 immediately

---

## Step 2 — Exchange Code for Access Token

Run this immediately after getting the code:
```powershell
$code = "PASTE_YOUR_CODE_HERE"
$clientId = "YOUR_CLIENT_ID"
$clientSecret = "YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET"
$redirectUri = "YOUR_REDIRECT_URI"

$body = @{
    grant_type    = "authorization_code"
    code          = $code
    client_id     = $clientId
    client_secret = $clientSecret
    redirect_uri  = $redirectUri
}

$token = Invoke-RestMethod -Method POST -Uri "https://www.linkedin.com/oauth/v2/accessToken" -Body $body -ContentType "application/x-www-form-urlencoded"
$token
```

**Successful output looks like:**
```
access_token : AQVa7-O47q8HZBes......
expires_in   : 5183999
scope        : email,openid,profile,w_member_social
token_type   : Bearer
id_token     : eyJraWQi......
```

- `access_token` → use this to make API calls (valid for ~60 days)
- `expires_in` → seconds until token expires
- `scope` → permissions granted

---

## Step 3 — Get Your Profile and URN

```powershell
$accessToken = $token.access_token
$headers = @{ Authorization = "Bearer $accessToken" }
$u = Invoke-RestMethod -Method GET -Uri "https://api.linkedin.com/v2/userinfo" -Headers $headers
$u
```

**Output looks like:**
```
sub            : 12d232226Z
name           : User name
given_name     : user nume
family_name    : family name
picture        : https://media.licdn.com/...
email          : email@example.com
email_verified : True
locale         : en_US
```

- `$u.sub` → this is your LinkedIn member ID
- Your full URN = `urn:li:person:` + `$u.sub`
- Example: `urn:li:person:12d232226Z`

To print your URN:
```powershell
"urn:li:person:$($u.sub)"
```

---

## Common Errors and Fixes

**400 Bad Request on token exchange**
- Code expired — you took more than 60 seconds
- Code already used — each code works only once
- Fix: go back to Step 1 and get a fresh code immediately

**Invalid state format**
- This happens when n8n intercepts the redirect
- n8n replaces `state=123456` with its own encrypted state token
- The error page is from n8n, not LinkedIn — ignore it
- The code in the URL is still valid — copy it and use it

**`u` is not recognized**
- You forgot the `$` sign
- Always use `$u` not `u`

**401 Unauthorized on API call**
- You used `$accessToken` before setting it from `$token.access_token`
- Fix: run `$accessToken = $token.access_token` first

**Scope errors / missing permissions**
- You requested a scope your app doesn't have access to
- Fix: go to your app's Products tab and request the needed product

---

## Summary of what each value is and where it comes from

| Value | What it is | Where to get it |
|---|---|---|
| Client ID | Your app's public identifier | LinkedIn Developer App → Auth tab |
| Client Secret | Your app's private key | LinkedIn Developer App → Auth tab |
| Redirect URI | Where LinkedIn sends the code | You define it in Auth tab |
| Code | Temporary one-time auth code | From the redirect URL after login |
| Access Token | Key to call the API | Returned after exchanging the code |
| URN | Your LinkedIn member ID | `$u.sub` from userinfo API |
